      <h1>I rebuilt the security labs from scratch</h1>
      <div class="blog-post-meta">
        <span class="blog-post-date">March 26, 2026</span>
        <span class="blog-post-tag">labs</span>
        <span class="blog-post-tag">architecture</span>
        <span class="blog-post-tag">SOAR</span>
        <span class="blog-post-read">10 min read</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="blog-article-lede">The first version of the security labs worked. You could exploit XSS, investigate a breach, get some XP. But it didn't feel like security work. The red team labs were a checklist — type the payload, watch a regex match it, get a green checkmark. The blue team labs were worse — five hardcoded alerts, click a classification button, submit. I've spent seven years on a CIRT team. I know what real incident response feels like. That wasn't it.</p>

    <div class="blog-body">
      <h2>What was wrong</h2>
      <p>Two things bothered me. First, the red team labs didn't validate whether your exploit actually <em>worked</em>. They checked if your input <em>looked like</em> an exploit. Type <code>&lt;img src=x onerror=alert(1)&gt;</code> into a search box, a regex matches <code>/onerror\s*=/i</code>, and the objective completes. The XSS never needed to fire. The JavaScript never needed to execute. You were passing a pattern test, not exploiting a vulnerability.</p>
      <p>Second, the blue team labs had nothing to investigate. Five alerts in a list. Click the right button. Submit. Where's the 200-entry log corpus you have to dig through? Where's the KQL search, the multi-source correlation, the moment where you pivot on an IP and suddenly the whole attack chain clicks into place? The labs felt like a quiz, not like sitting in a SOC at 2 AM working an active incident.</p>

      <h2>The simulation engine</h2>
      <p>The core of the rebuild is a new simulation engine that replaces regex pattern matching with real validation. When you craft an XSS payload in the terminal and send it to the ShopStack Worker, the Worker reflects it in the HTML response. The browser renders it. If JavaScript executes, the page's overridden <code>window.alert</code> catches it and sends a postMessage to the parent frame. If you access <code>document.cookie</code>, a wrapped getter reports that too. The simulation engine sees these messages and transitions its state machine.</p>
      <p>Nothing completes until the exploit actually works. You can type the right payload all day — if it doesn't fire in the browser context, the lab doesn't progress.</p>

      <pre><code>// In the ShopStack Worker — we override alert to detect XSS execution
var _origAlert = window.alert;
window.alert = function(msg) {
  window.parent.postMessage({ type: 'xss-fired', executed: true, payload: String(msg) }, '*');
};

// And wrap document.cookie to detect exfiltration
Object.defineProperty(document, 'cookie', {
  get: function() {
    window.parent.postMessage({ type: 'cookie-accessed', value: _origCookie }, '*');
    return _origCookie;
  }
});</code></pre>

      <p>Same principle for every lab. SQL injection against VaultBank validates that the query returned unauthorized data. SSRF on CloudSnap validates that an internal endpoint was actually fetched. JWT algorithm confusion on DevPortal validates that a forged token was accepted and returned admin-scoped data. The Workers are the source of truth — they know whether the exploit succeeded.</p>

      <h2>The red team workstation</h2>
      <p>The old labs had a text box and a shoddy console. The new red team workstation is a three-panel layout with actual tools.</p>
      <p>The left panel is your mission brief — a pen test engagement narrative, not a numbered checklist. "You've been retained to prove exploitability and demonstrate business impact. The client needs a PoC that shows real data exposure — not just an alert box." Your job is to figure out how.</p>
      <p>The center panel has tabbed tools: a terminal emulator that makes real <code>curl</code> requests to the Worker and displays the actual HTTP response, a Burp-style request repeater for crafting custom requests, an encoder/decoder for payload manipulation, and a scratchpad for notes. The terminal isn't simulated — <code>curl</code> uses <code>fetch()</code> against the live Worker and you see the real response headers, the real HTML body, the real reflected input.</p>
      <p>The right panel is a live browser view. The target app (ShopStack, VaultBank, whatever) renders in a sandboxed iframe. When your XSS payload fires, you see it happen — the alert intercept, the cookie value, the DOM change. The "aha" moment is visual and immediate.</p>

      <h2>The SOC workstation</h2>
      <p>This is the part I'm most proud of, because it's the part I have the most opinions about.</p>
      <p>The blue team labs now drop you into an active incident with a SIEM containing hundreds of log entries from six sources — WAF, web server, firewall, IDS, authentication, DNS. You get a case file from your SOC lead with open-ended objectives: reconstruct the attack timeline, identify all compromised assets, recommend containment. No numbered steps. No checkboxes. Figure it out.</p>
      <p>The SIEM has real tools. KQL-style search. Source and severity filtering. Time range selection. Every log entry expands to show the full event detail with raw JSON. And every entry has action buttons: Pivot on IP, Add to Evidence, Lookup in Threat Intel, Pin to Timeline.</p>
      <p>Those buttons do things. Pivot on IP filters every log source to show all activity from that address. Threat Intel lookup pulls reputation data on IPs and domains. Pin to Timeline builds a visual attack timeline in the right panel. Add to Evidence lights up a piece in the case file's evidence tracker — which shows you what you've found and how much is still undiscovered.</p>

      <div class="blog-callout blog-callout-insight">
        <strong>The evidence design is deliberate.</strong> The ShopStack investigation lab has 306 log entries. About 36 of them are the actual attack chain. The rest are normal traffic — legitimate users browsing products, health check pings, CDN cache hits, a couple of WAF false positives on search queries containing the word "where." You can't just read through all 306 entries. You have to search, filter, correlate, and pivot. That's what investigation actually is.
      </div>

      <h2>Multiple valid paths</h2>
      <p>Real investigations don't have a single correct order. Some analysts start with the alert that fired and work backward. Some start with the attacker IP and work forward. Some check DNS first, some check auth logs first. All of them get to the same answer.</p>
      <p>The investigation state machine supports this. It tracks which evidence you've discovered, not what order you discovered it in. You can find the XSS payload first and then trace the session hijack, or find the session hijack first and then trace it back to the XSS. Both paths light up the same evidence pieces. The lab completes when you've found enough evidence and submitted a containment plan — not when you've clicked the right things in the right order.</p>

      <h2>Scoring that measures skill, not speed</h2>
      <p>The old labs gave you a flat score with XP penalties for using hints. The new system scores across four axes:</p>
      <p><strong>Technique</strong> — did you use the right tools and methods? Using <code>curl</code> to confirm a reflection point before crafting a payload scores higher than pasting random payloads until something works. Correlating across three log sources scores higher than only checking one.</p>
      <p><strong>Efficiency</strong> — how direct was your path? Six purposeful actions to achieve the objective versus forty random attempts. This isn't about speed — it's about signal-to-noise in your own approach.</p>
      <p><strong>Thoroughness</strong> — did you find everything? Some evidence is buried. Some requires pivoting through multiple sources. Bonus evidence exists for users who dig deeper than the minimum.</p>
      <p><strong>Independence</strong> — how much scaffolding did you need? The adaptive hint system shows contextual nudges when you're stuck, but using fewer nudges scores higher. At level 7 and above, nudges stop appearing entirely. You've earned the right to be left alone.</p>
      <p>Each axis scores 0-100, and the composite determines your XP. More importantly, you can see the individual axes — so you know that you're efficient but not thorough, or thorough but not independent. That's specific, actionable feedback on how to improve.</p>

      <h2>Cross-perspective debriefs</h2>
      <p>After completing a red team lab, the debrief shows you "What a Defender Sees" — the WAF alerts your attack generated, the suspicious outbound connections, the impossible-travel session usage. After completing a blue team lab, it shows "What the Attacker Did" — the exact recon, the payload crafting, the exploitation chain.</p>
      <p>This is the feature that doesn't exist anywhere else. TryHackMe and HackTheBox teach offense. CyberDefenders and LetsDefend teach defense. Nobody teaches both on the same incident. But you can't be a good defender without understanding how attackers think, and you can't be a good attacker without understanding what defenders see. The red-to-blue connection makes both sides deeper.</p>

      <h2>Procedural generation</h2>
      <p>The first version had a spoilability problem. Once someone publishes "the attacker IP is 198.51.100.47" on a walkthrough site, the investigation lab becomes a lookup exercise. The new version randomizes surface-level details on every run.</p>
      <p>Attacker IPs get pulled from realistic VPS hosting ranges. Timestamps shift by a random offset. Victim usernames and email addresses change. Exfiltration domains are generated by a DGA-like algorithm. Session tokens are regenerated. And 15-40 extra noise entries from a curated traffic library are injected at random positions in the log corpus.</p>
      <p>The attack chain logic stays identical — you're still investigating the same XSS-to-session-hijack-to-data-export chain. But the specific indicators are different every time. You can't google the answer. You have to actually investigate.</p>

      <h2>Certificates</h2>
      <p>Complete all beginner red team labs and earn "Red Team Fundamentals." Complete all blue team labs and earn "Incident Response Practitioner." Complete all twelve and earn "Full Spectrum Operator."</p>
      <p>Each certificate includes a SHA-256 verification hash derived from the completed lab IDs and the timestamp. The hash is stored in your encrypted progress alongside your Forge Key. You can share the hash to prove completion without revealing your identity or progress data. Privacy-first all the way down.</p>

      <h2>What I didn't build</h2>
      <p>Accounts. Email verification. OAuth. Password reset flows. Cookie consent banners. GDPR data export endpoints. I didn't build any of it because I don't need any of it. Your progress lives in <code>localStorage</code>. If you want to sync it across devices, your Forge Key encrypts it with AES-256-GCM before it touches my infrastructure. I literally cannot see your data.</p>
      <p>The Intercept tool (Burp Proxy-style request modification) is also missing from the red team workstation. Intercepting cross-origin requests from a sandboxed iframe requires a Service Worker or custom proxy layer that I haven't built yet. It's coming.</p>

      <h2>The numbers</h2>
      <p>12 labs. 6 red team (XSS, SQL injection, SSRF, JWT, race condition, IDOR). 6 blue team (alert triage, breach investigation, phishing analysis, log analysis, threat containment, SOAR playbook). 6 Cloudflare Workers serving production-quality target applications with real vulnerabilities. 750+ log entries across 6 investigation scenarios. 17 JavaScript modules. Zero signup. Zero telemetry. Zero cost.</p>

      <h2>Try it</h2>
      <p>The labs are live at <a href="/labs/">penumbraforge.com/labs</a>. Start with RED-01 (XSS on ShopStack) to see the new attacker workstation, then switch to BLUE-02 (Investigate ShopStack) to see the same attack from the defender's perspective.</p>
      <p>If you're a SOC team lead looking for training material that doesn't require a procurement process, this is it. If you're learning security and tired of regex-matching your way through platforms that call it "hands-on," this is something different.</p>
      <p>Source is on <a href="https://github.com/penumbraforge">GitHub</a>. If something's broken or you have ideas for new labs, let me know.</p>
